A||N||4||Thor||Headlight/Tail Light Problem||||1||
Z||000000||Thor||07-14-2000||02:50 AM||||Could someone please tell me if the following is eather an isolation switch or a grownding problem? I own a 86 Fiero GT.  Headlights go up...I blow the 20amp fuse. However!  If I simply turn on the parking lights...I blow the 20amp fuse.  I'v brought the the car to my local Pontiac dealer thinking if anyone can fix the problem they can?...NOT!  they replaced a relay three times now and have finally told me that the passenger side headlight motor was "tired" and it needs to be replaced. When I reminded them that I'm blowing the fuse just by actuating the parking lights I was told THAT circit runs through the same relay?...Well if so does'nt the problem point to the relay?...Now I've learned there are three different relays for this year fiero.  If it's a relay...which one?...If It's a short...where? Or are they right and I need a new headlight motor? {The right headlight does lag a-bit apon actuation}. P.S I'v owned this car since 1991 and have put 100,000 miles on her for a total of 187000 miles and she still runs and looks beautiful!!...THANKS FOR ANY HELP IN ADVANCE. ||||reg||1
Z||000001||StuGood||07-14-2000||12:36 PM||||Sounds like a short in the wiring to the parking light / tail light to me.  NOT the headlight motor, and not a relay. <P>If headlight motor is sluggish, you can (and probably should) rebuild it.  Much less expensive than replacement.<P>For what it's worth, there ARE indeed three relays associated with the headlight motors - one mounted on each motor, plus an isolation relay.     ||||reg||3
Z||000002||lawrence||07-14-2000||12:39 PM||||Everyone has their own way to troubleshoot things, so it's tough to give you step-by-step instructions.  It's particularly difficult to fix electrical problems remotely.<P>One way is to start pulling things to see if the problem goes away.  You can start clean and disconnect everything, or you can pull things one at the time.  The former will save you fuses.<P>If the problem is when you put the parking lights on, start there.  Pull all the bulbs out and see if you still have a problem.  If not, then you have a shorted lamp/socket.  If you still have the problem, then there's a short in the system wiring.  Do you have an ohmmeter?  You can get a very inexpensive one from Radio Shack for about $10.  It isn't the best unit available, but it's worth it's weight in gold for just these kinds of problems.<P>Good luck,<P>-L<BR>  <A HREF="http://members.nccw.net/webe" TARGET=_blank>http://members.nccw.net/webe</A>  - WebElectric Magazine||||reg||1
Z||000003||Taijiguy||07-14-2000||12:41 PM||||I'd start troubleshooting the parking light circuit. The headlights are on a circuit breaker, and the motors use fusible links. So there are no fuses used in either of those circuits at all. The parking lights use a 20 amp fuse, but no relays are in that circuit at all. The parking lights are controlled by the switch alone. <BR>I would probably start by inspecting all the sockets and bulbs, since they are most vulnerable to corrosion, and could create a short. Or, if you've only started experiencing this, and recently had (or did) some type of work, electrical or otherwise, I would look in the area of that service for potential causes. But according to the schematics in Haynes, there are no relays in the parking lite circuit at all. None. Zero. Zilch.<P>Good luck!<P>-Taiji<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||sdm1964||07-15-2000||09:43 AM||||The book is wrong, the parking lights do go thru the isolation relay. I would try replacing that first. I had one that stuck and the parking lights and headlights would stay on. I looked at the wiring diagram too, It is totally wrong.||||reg||14
